1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,106
December twenty-third, two thousand eight.

2
00:00:03,106 --> 00:00:06,852
For months, Elon Musk has watched his money run out.

3
00:00:06,852 --> 00:00:11,391
His rocket company, SpaceX, has failed three launches in a row.

4
00:00:11,530 --> 00:00:15,738
His electric car company, Tesla, is about to miss payroll.

5
00:00:15,738 --> 00:00:20,307
And the world is sunk in the worst financial crisis in decades.

6
00:00:20,553 --> 00:00:23,082
That day, NASA announces a contract:

7
00:00:23,082 --> 00:00:28,843
about one point six billion dollars for SpaceX to carry cargo to the International

8
00:00:28,843 --> 00:00:29,826
Space Station.

9
00:00:30,098 --> 00:00:31,770
The next day, at six p.m.

10
00:00:31,770 --> 00:00:35,580
on Christmas Eve, Tesla closes the funding that saves it.

11
00:00:35,580 --> 00:00:39,659
According to Musk, in the last hour of the last possible day.

12
00:00:39,739 --> 00:00:45,088
But to understand why a United States government agency ended up rescuing a private

13
00:00:45,088 --> 00:00:49,535
company whose rocket kept blowing up, we have to go back fifty years.

14
00:00:51,069 --> 00:00:51,858
To a beep.

15
00:00:51,858 --> 00:00:58,488
A beep that came from space in nineteen fifty-seven and terrified an entire country.

16
00:00:58,707 --> 00:01:01,471
This is the story of how NASA was born.

17
00:01:01,471 --> 00:01:07,071
And how, half a century later, the agency created to beat the Soviets in a race

18
00:01:07,071 --> 00:01:09,835
ended up depending on a private rocket.

19
00:01:10,159 --> 00:01:13,513
And at the end, I'll ask you an uncomfortable question:

20
00:01:13,513 --> 00:01:16,807
after everything that happened, who owes more to whom?

21
00:01:17,457 --> 00:01:18,533
Chapter one:

22
00:01:18,533 --> 00:01:20,147
a beep in the sky.

23
00:01:20,708 --> 00:01:23,328
October fourth, nineteen fifty-seven.

24
00:01:23,328 --> 00:01:29,277
The Soviet Union announces it has put the first artificial satellite in history into

25
00:01:29,277 --> 00:01:29,702
orbit:

26
00:01:29,702 --> 00:01:30,269
Sputnik.

27
00:01:31,455 --> 00:01:36,755
It's a polished metal sphere fifty-eight centimeters wide, with four long antennas.

28
00:01:36,755 --> 00:01:38,990
It weighs about eighty-three kilos.

29
00:01:39,070 --> 00:01:40,847
And it barely does anything:

30
00:01:40,847 --> 00:01:42,814
it just sends out a radio beep.

31
00:01:42,814 --> 00:01:46,811
But anyone with a receiver could hear it as it passed overhead.

32
00:01:46,891 --> 00:01:50,797
It circled the Earth in just over an hour and a half.

33
00:01:50,797 --> 00:01:53,892
Again and again, day and night, for weeks.

34
00:01:54,865 --> 00:01:58,198
In the United States, the news lands like a bomb.

35
00:01:58,198 --> 00:02:03,638
If the Soviets could launch a metal ball into space, they could launch something

36
00:02:03,638 --> 00:02:04,387
much worse.

37
00:02:04,467 --> 00:02:08,310
In the middle of the Cold War, the fear was easy to understand:

38
00:02:08,310 --> 00:02:13,434
the same rocket that puts a satellite in orbit can carry an atomic bomb to the other

39
00:02:13,434 --> 00:02:14,532
side of the world.

40
00:02:14,612 --> 00:02:17,703
A month later, the Soviets do it again.

41
00:02:17,703 --> 00:02:21,111
On November third, they launch Sputnik two.

42
00:02:21,111 --> 00:02:23,330
This time, with a passenger:

43
00:02:23,330 --> 00:02:24,757
a dog named Laika.

44
00:02:24,837 --> 00:02:26,964
Laika didn't survive the trip.

45
00:02:26,964 --> 00:02:30,934
But the message for the United States was crystal clear:

46
00:02:30,934 --> 00:02:32,565
the Soviets were ahead.

47
00:02:32,565 --> 00:02:33,274
Way ahead.

48
00:02:33,354 --> 00:02:38,248
Congress opens hearings to find out how the richest country in the world had fallen

49
00:02:38,248 --> 00:02:38,661
behind.

50
00:02:38,661 --> 00:02:41,609
The press starts talking about the Sputnik crisis.

51
00:02:42,408 --> 00:02:45,161
The United States had to respond fast.

52
00:02:45,161 --> 00:02:46,754
It had a rocket ready.

53
00:02:46,754 --> 00:02:51,028
And it decided to show it to the world… live on television.

54
00:02:51,482 --> 00:02:52,673
Chapter two:

55
00:02:52,673 --> 00:02:53,467
Flopnik.

56
00:02:54,184 --> 00:02:56,798
December sixth, nineteen fifty-seven.

57
00:02:56,798 --> 00:02:58,494
Cape Canaveral, Florida.

58
00:02:58,494 --> 00:03:03,510
The Vanguard rocket waits on the pad with a small satellite on its tip.

59
00:03:04,278 --> 00:03:06,788
Television cameras broadcast it live.

60
00:03:06,788 --> 00:03:08,823
The whole country is watching.

61
00:03:08,990 --> 00:03:09,907
Countdown.

62
00:03:09,907 --> 00:03:10,732
Ignition.

63
00:03:10,732 --> 00:03:14,032
The rocket rises… just over a meter.

64
00:03:14,251 --> 00:03:19,515
It loses thrust, falls back onto the pad, and explodes in a huge fireball.

65
00:03:19,595 --> 00:03:25,445
The little satellite was thrown clear and, as was later told, ended up lying in the

66
00:03:25,445 --> 00:03:27,771
bushes… still sending its signal.

67
00:03:28,039 --> 00:03:29,824
The press showed no mercy.

68
00:03:29,824 --> 00:03:33,188
They gave it nicknames like Flopnik and Kaputnik.

69
00:03:33,188 --> 00:03:37,377
The New York Times called it a blow to the nation's prestige.

70
00:03:37,457 --> 00:03:42,084
And a senator from Texas named Lyndon Johnson, who would later become president,

71
00:03:42,084 --> 00:03:44,745
called it humiliating for the American people.

72
00:03:45,979 --> 00:03:49,026
But eight weeks later comes the comeback.

73
00:03:49,026 --> 00:03:54,821
On January thirty-first, nineteen fifty-eight, the Army launches Explorer one.

74
00:03:54,901 --> 00:03:59,486
The rocket was built by the team of German engineer Wernher von Braun.

75
00:03:59,486 --> 00:04:03,809
And the satellite, by the Jet Propulsion Laboratory in California.

76
00:04:04,260 --> 00:04:07,615
And Explorer one did something Sputnik hadn't:

77
00:04:07,615 --> 00:04:09,876
it made a scientific discovery.

78
00:04:09,876 --> 00:04:15,346
It detected belts of radiation around the Earth that today bear the name of

79
00:04:15,346 --> 00:04:17,243
scientist James Van Allen.

80
00:04:17,749 --> 00:04:22,894
In March comes Vanguard one, the first satellite powered by solar energy.

81
00:04:22,894 --> 00:04:25,008
And here's an incredible fact:

82
00:04:25,008 --> 00:04:26,417
it's still in orbit.

83
00:04:26,417 --> 00:04:29,870
It's the oldest human-made object still up there.

84
00:04:31,970 --> 00:04:34,375
The United States now had satellites.

85
00:04:34,375 --> 00:04:36,194
But it had a bigger problem:

86
00:04:36,194 --> 00:04:40,742
every branch of the government and the military was going its own way.

87
00:04:40,742 --> 00:04:42,432
It needed a single agency.

88
00:04:43,082 --> 00:04:44,287
Chapter three:

89
00:04:44,287 --> 00:04:45,407
NASA is born.

90
00:04:46,098 --> 00:04:48,742
July twenty-ninth, nineteen fifty-eight.

91
00:04:48,742 --> 00:04:53,569
President Dwight Eisenhower signs the National Aeronautics and Space Act.

92
00:04:55,721 --> 00:04:57,407
That's how NASA is born:

93
00:04:57,407 --> 00:05:00,920
the National Aeronautics and Space Administration.

94
00:05:01,087 --> 00:05:03,245
And it's born with a key decision:

95
00:05:03,245 --> 00:05:06,419
it would be a civilian agency, not a military one.

96
00:05:06,419 --> 00:05:10,608
At least in public, space would be explored for peaceful purposes.

97
00:05:10,688 --> 00:05:13,336
On October first, NASA opens its doors.

98
00:05:13,336 --> 00:05:15,644
But it doesn't start from scratch:

99
00:05:15,644 --> 00:05:21,346
it absorbs an agency that had spent decades researching airplanes, the NACA, founded

100
00:05:21,346 --> 00:05:22,704
in nineteen fifteen.

101
00:05:22,784 --> 00:05:27,594
With it come about eight thousand employees, several laboratories, and a budget of

102
00:05:27,594 --> 00:05:29,881
about a hundred million dollars a year.

103
00:05:29,961 --> 00:05:35,275
Its first administrator is Keith Glennan, president of a university in Ohio.

104
00:05:35,275 --> 00:05:37,303
He accepted on one condition:

105
00:05:37,303 --> 00:05:41,359
that the head of the NACA, Hugh Dryden, be his number two.

106
00:05:41,439 --> 00:05:47,610
In April nineteen fifty-nine, it introduces its first seven astronauts, the Mercury

107
00:05:47,610 --> 00:05:48,205
program.

108
00:05:48,205 --> 00:05:52,890
The country welcomed them as heroes before they had even flown.

109
00:05:52,970 --> 00:05:55,388
One of them was named Gordon Cooper.

110
00:05:55,388 --> 00:05:56,664
Remember that name:

111
00:05:56,664 --> 00:06:00,023
it will come back in this story fifty years later.

112
00:06:00,891 --> 00:06:02,954
But the Soviets kept winning.

113
00:06:02,954 --> 00:06:08,858
On April twelfth, nineteen sixty-one, Yuri Gagarin becomes the first human being to

114
00:06:08,858 --> 00:06:11,418
travel to space and orbit the Earth.

115
00:06:13,075 --> 00:06:16,762
The United States responds three weeks later with Alan Shepard.

116
00:06:16,762 --> 00:06:20,859
But his flight lasts fifteen minutes and doesn't go around the planet.

117
00:06:21,549 --> 00:06:22,951
Then President John F.

118
00:06:22,951 --> 00:06:24,545
Kennedy makes a huge bet:

119
00:06:24,545 --> 00:06:29,072
land a man on the Moon and bring him back before the end of the decade.

120
00:06:30,989 --> 00:06:34,197
To pull it off, NASA grows like never before.

121
00:06:34,197 --> 00:06:39,685
In nineteen sixty-six, it spends more than four percent of the entire federal

122
00:06:39,685 --> 00:06:40,184
budget.

123
00:06:40,378 --> 00:06:45,513
About four hundred thousand people worked on the Apollo program, between NASA

124
00:06:45,513 --> 00:06:47,248
employees and contractors.

125
00:06:48,486 --> 00:06:52,644
And notice that detail, because it's the seed of everything to come:

126
00:06:52,644 --> 00:06:55,212
NASA almost never built its rockets alone.

127
00:06:55,212 --> 00:06:59,431
It designed them and paid for them, but private companies built them.

128
00:06:59,833 --> 00:07:05,183
And on July twentieth, nineteen sixty-nine, the United States wins the race:

129
00:07:05,183 --> 00:07:07,435
Apollo eleven lands on the Moon.

130
00:07:08,020 --> 00:07:13,516
The agency born out of fear of a Soviet beep had taken humanity to another world in

131
00:07:13,516 --> 00:07:14,707
just eleven years.

132
00:07:15,501 --> 00:07:17,592
But winning the race had a problem:

133
00:07:17,592 --> 00:07:21,057
when there's no rival anymore, the urgency disappears too.

134
00:07:21,057 --> 00:07:22,372
And so does the money.

135
00:07:23,034 --> 00:07:23,962
Chapter four:

136
00:07:23,962 --> 00:07:25,960
the price of going to space.

137
00:07:26,677 --> 00:07:29,505
After Apollo, NASA's budget collapses.

138
00:07:29,505 --> 00:07:34,193
Its big bet for the following decades is a reusable spacecraft:

139
00:07:34,193 --> 00:07:35,533
the Space Shuttle.

140
00:07:37,189 --> 00:07:40,746
It flies for the first time in nineteen eighty-one.

141
00:07:40,746 --> 00:07:45,419
The idea was for going to space to be almost like boarding a plane:

142
00:07:45,419 --> 00:07:46,535
quick and cheap.

143
00:07:46,535 --> 00:07:47,232
It wasn't.

144
00:07:47,312 --> 00:07:48,564
And it cost lives.

145
00:07:48,564 --> 00:07:51,624
In nineteen eighty-six, Challenger explodes.

146
00:07:51,624 --> 00:07:56,493
In two thousand three, Columbia breaks apart on its way back to Earth.

147
00:07:56,493 --> 00:07:58,162
Fourteen astronauts die.

148
00:07:58,242 --> 00:08:02,136
After Columbia, the United States decides to retire the shuttle.

149
00:08:02,136 --> 00:08:06,882
And NASA has to figure out how it will keep taking cargo and astronauts to the

150
00:08:06,882 --> 00:08:08,586
International Space Station.

151
00:08:08,959 --> 00:08:13,425
In two thousand six, it tries something that would have sounded crazy in the

152
00:08:13,425 --> 00:08:13,895
fifties:

153
00:08:13,895 --> 00:08:18,303
instead of owning the spacecraft, it will pay private companies for hitting

154
00:08:18,303 --> 00:08:18,950
milestones.

155
00:08:19,030 --> 00:08:20,933
The program is called COTS.

156
00:08:20,933 --> 00:08:24,529
In August two thousand six, it picks two companies.

157
00:08:24,529 --> 00:08:28,688
One of them gets two hundred seventy-eight million dollars.

158
00:08:28,688 --> 00:08:30,027
It's called SpaceX.

159
00:08:30,107 --> 00:08:35,655
The other one, Rocketplane Kistler, couldn't raise the money it needed and ended up

160
00:08:35,655 --> 00:08:36,925
out of the program.

161
00:08:37,097 --> 00:08:42,379
To understand who was behind SpaceX, we have to travel to Moscow, in the fall of two

162
00:08:42,379 --> 00:08:43,197
thousand one.

163
00:08:43,847 --> 00:08:44,761
Chapter five:

164
00:08:44,761 --> 00:08:47,504
a millionaire who wanted to go to Mars.

165
00:08:47,751 --> 00:08:52,915
Elon Musk was thirty and had gotten rich with PayPal, the online payments company

166
00:08:52,915 --> 00:08:55,274
that eBay bought in two thousand two.

167
00:08:56,303 --> 00:08:58,279
And he had a strange idea:

168
00:08:58,279 --> 00:09:04,512
send a small greenhouse to Mars to grow plants there, and spark public interest in

169
00:09:04,512 --> 00:09:05,424
space again.

170
00:09:05,424 --> 00:09:07,248
He called it Mars Oasis.

171
00:09:08,774 --> 00:09:10,539
The problem was the rocket:

172
00:09:10,539 --> 00:09:13,220
in the United States they cost a fortune.

173
00:09:13,220 --> 00:09:18,713
So in October two thousand one, he flew to Moscow to buy converted Russian ballistic

174
00:09:18,713 --> 00:09:19,301
missiles.

175
00:09:19,381 --> 00:09:23,887
He went twice, in October two thousand one and February two thousand two.

176
00:09:23,887 --> 00:09:26,665
And both times he came back without a rocket.

177
00:09:26,745 --> 00:09:32,533
As he later told it, on the flight home he ran the numbers and reached a conclusion:

178
00:09:32,533 --> 00:09:36,736
a rocket's materials cost only a small fraction of its price.

179
00:09:36,736 --> 00:09:38,665
He could build them himself.

180
00:09:38,745 --> 00:09:42,909
In two thousand two, he founds Space Exploration Technologies:

181
00:09:42,909 --> 00:09:43,379
SpaceX.

182
00:09:43,379 --> 00:09:46,132
Its first rocket will be small and cheap:

183
00:09:46,132 --> 00:09:47,139
the Falcon one.

184
00:09:47,219 --> 00:09:49,961
To launch it, they pick an unusual place:

185
00:09:49,961 --> 00:09:55,378
Omelek, a tiny island in the Kwajalein Atoll, in the middle of the Pacific Ocean.

186
00:09:55,458 --> 00:10:00,583
Almost every expert thought a private company couldn't reach orbit on its own.

187
00:10:00,583 --> 00:10:04,000
And for years, the facts seemed to prove them right.

188
00:10:05,834 --> 00:10:06,851
Chapter six:

189
00:10:06,851 --> 00:10:08,290
three explosions.

190
00:10:08,955 --> 00:10:11,677
March twenty-fourth, two thousand six.

191
00:10:11,677 --> 00:10:13,682
The first Falcon one launch.

192
00:10:13,682 --> 00:10:15,831
Seconds later, an engine fire.

193
00:10:15,831 --> 00:10:17,980
The rocket falls into the sea.

194
00:10:18,060 --> 00:10:20,695
March twenty-first, two thousand seven.

195
00:10:20,695 --> 00:10:21,709
Second attempt.

196
00:10:21,709 --> 00:10:27,250
This time it reaches space, but the second stage starts to wobble and doesn't make

197
00:10:27,250 --> 00:10:27,655
orbit.

198
00:10:27,735 --> 00:10:30,165
August second, two thousand eight.

199
00:10:30,165 --> 00:10:31,165
Third attempt.

200
00:10:31,165 --> 00:10:34,452
And this time the rocket carries real payload:

201
00:10:34,452 --> 00:10:38,167
a military satellite and two small NASA experiments.

202
00:10:38,247 --> 00:10:40,518
The first stage works perfectly.

203
00:10:40,518 --> 00:10:45,273
But during separation, a timing error makes the two stages collide.

204
00:10:45,273 --> 00:10:46,267
Third failure.

205
00:10:47,005 --> 00:10:52,279
Also on board was a capsule with the ashes of more than two hundred people who had

206
00:10:52,279 --> 00:10:53,822
wanted to rest in space.

207
00:10:54,355 --> 00:11:00,081
Among them were the ashes of the actor who played Scotty in Star Trek… and those of

208
00:11:00,081 --> 00:11:03,668
Gordon Cooper, one of NASA's first seven astronauts.

209
00:11:03,748 --> 00:11:09,098
Half a century after NASA introduced its first astronauts, the ashes of one of them

210
00:11:09,098 --> 00:11:12,708
were lost on a private rocket that couldn't reach orbit.

211
00:11:14,595 --> 00:11:18,069
And meanwhile, Musk's other company was sinking too.

212
00:11:19,803 --> 00:11:21,013
Chapter seven:

213
00:11:21,013 --> 00:11:22,311
the worst year.

214
00:11:22,793 --> 00:11:28,162
Tesla had spent years trying to build its first car, an electric sports car called

215
00:11:28,162 --> 00:11:29,013
the Roadster.

216
00:11:29,013 --> 00:11:32,549
It was behind schedule and cost far more than planned.

217
00:11:32,629 --> 00:11:36,116
In October two thousand eight, Musk takes over as Tesla's CEO.

218
00:11:36,116 --> 00:11:40,785
He lays off part of the staff and puts in his own money so the company doesn't shut

219
00:11:40,785 --> 00:11:41,066
down.

220
00:11:41,146 --> 00:11:44,191
And in September, Lehman Brothers had collapsed.

221
00:11:44,191 --> 00:11:48,124
Banks weren't lending and investors didn't want to take risks.

222
00:11:48,124 --> 00:11:51,169
It was the worst possible time to ask for money.

223
00:11:51,249 --> 00:11:55,641
Musk has often recalled that year as one of the hardest of his life.

224
00:11:55,641 --> 00:11:59,451
He had two companies, and both could fall at the same time.

225
00:12:00,059 --> 00:12:05,631
As the story goes, SpaceX had just enough left for one last Falcon one launch.

226
00:12:05,631 --> 00:12:08,418
If it failed, it was probably all over.

227
00:12:08,585 --> 00:12:12,477
Everything depended on one rocket on a remote island in the Pacific.

228
00:12:13,114 --> 00:12:14,211
Chapter eight:

229
00:12:14,211 --> 00:12:15,700
the fourth attempt.

230
00:12:16,182 --> 00:12:19,489
September twenty-eighth, two thousand eight.

231
00:12:19,489 --> 00:12:20,015
Omelek.

232
00:12:20,015 --> 00:12:23,397
The fourth Falcon one carries a test payload:

233
00:12:23,397 --> 00:12:28,733
a block of metal of about a hundred sixty-five kilos, nicknamed RatSat.

234
00:12:28,813 --> 00:12:29,505
Liftoff.

235
00:12:29,505 --> 00:12:31,410
The first stage works.

236
00:12:31,410 --> 00:12:32,967
Separation… clean.

237
00:12:32,967 --> 00:12:35,997
The second stage lights its engine.

238
00:12:36,369 --> 00:12:39,579
And a few minutes later comes the confirmation:

239
00:12:39,579 --> 00:12:41,149
Falcon one is in orbit.

240
00:12:41,970 --> 00:12:47,387
It's the first privately developed liquid-fueled rocket to reach Earth orbit.

241
00:12:47,387 --> 00:12:50,694
At SpaceX, employees scream and hug each other.

242
00:12:52,716 --> 00:12:54,325
And look at the date:

243
00:12:54,325 --> 00:13:00,605
three days later, on October first, two thousand eight, NASA turned exactly fifty.

244
00:13:00,685 --> 00:13:03,528
But reaching orbit doesn't pay the bills.

245
00:13:03,528 --> 00:13:06,925
After the success, SpaceX still needed customers.

246
00:13:06,925 --> 00:13:08,935
And Tesla still needed money.

247
00:13:09,585 --> 00:13:10,572
Chapter nine:

248
00:13:10,572 --> 00:13:12,772
Christmas two thousand eight.

249
00:13:13,489 --> 00:13:15,298
December two thousand eight.

250
00:13:15,298 --> 00:13:19,432
Musk is splitting what little he has left between his companies.

251
00:13:19,432 --> 00:13:23,050
By his own account, he even borrowed money from friends.

252
00:13:23,130 --> 00:13:28,415
And then, on December twenty-third, NASA announces who will carry cargo to the

253
00:13:28,415 --> 00:13:32,007
International Space Station once the shuttle retires.

254
00:13:32,087 --> 00:13:37,183
It orders twelve flights from SpaceX for about one point six billion dollars.

255
00:13:37,183 --> 00:13:42,013
And eight flights from Orbital Sciences for about one point nine billion.

256
00:13:42,093 --> 00:13:46,843
For a company that, three months earlier, was one failure away from disappearing,

257
00:13:46,843 --> 00:13:48,250
that changed everything.

258
00:13:48,330 --> 00:13:53,703
And the next day, December twenty-fourth, Tesla closes a funding round of about

259
00:13:53,703 --> 00:13:55,200
forty million dollars.

260
00:13:56,356 --> 00:13:59,377
Years later, Musk said it closed at six p.m.

261
00:13:59,377 --> 00:14:02,399
on Christmas Eve, in the last possible hour.

262
00:14:02,399 --> 00:14:07,000
Otherwise, two days after Christmas, he couldn't have made payroll.

263
00:14:07,080 --> 00:14:11,845
In two days, both of his companies went from the brink of bankruptcy to having a

264
00:14:11,845 --> 00:14:12,262
future.

265
00:14:12,262 --> 00:14:14,168
And one of them owed it to NASA.

266
00:14:14,818 --> 00:14:15,740
Chapter ten:

267
00:14:15,740 --> 00:14:17,352
paying back the debt.

268
00:14:18,121 --> 00:14:21,162
What came next changed the space industry.

269
00:14:21,162 --> 00:14:27,170
In December two thousand ten, SpaceX becomes the first private company to recover a

270
00:14:27,170 --> 00:14:28,762
spacecraft from orbit:

271
00:14:28,762 --> 00:14:30,138
the Dragon capsule.

272
00:14:30,218 --> 00:14:34,761
In May two thousand twelve, a Dragon reaches the International Space Station.

273
00:14:34,761 --> 00:14:37,475
It's the first commercial spacecraft to do so.

274
00:14:37,555 --> 00:14:42,395
And in December two thousand fifteen, it pulls off something that seemed like

275
00:14:42,395 --> 00:14:43,401
science fiction:

276
00:14:43,401 --> 00:14:47,046
landing the first stage of an orbital rocket after launch.

277
00:14:47,126 --> 00:14:50,119
Meanwhile, NASA was in an awkward position.

278
00:14:50,119 --> 00:14:55,896
Since the shuttle retired, in two thousand eleven, it had no way of its own to send

279
00:14:55,896 --> 00:14:57,288
astronauts to space.

280
00:14:57,738 --> 00:15:02,602
To get them to the station, it paid Russia for seats on Soyuz spacecraft.

281
00:15:02,602 --> 00:15:07,665
The most expensive ones cost about eighty-six million dollars per astronaut.

282
00:15:08,616 --> 00:15:13,943
The agency born to beat the Soviets in the space race had to buy its rides from

283
00:15:13,943 --> 00:15:14,415
Russia.

284
00:15:15,731 --> 00:15:21,578
In two thousand fourteen, NASA awards the contracts to launch astronauts from home

285
00:15:21,578 --> 00:15:22,006
again:

286
00:15:22,006 --> 00:15:25,499
four point two billion for Boeing, the old giant.

287
00:15:25,499 --> 00:15:27,852
Two point six billion for SpaceX.

288
00:15:30,187 --> 00:15:35,782
And on May thirtieth, two thousand twenty, two NASA astronauts lift off from Florida

289
00:15:35,782 --> 00:15:36,914
in a Crew Dragon.

290
00:15:36,914 --> 00:15:41,310
It's the first crewed launch from the United States in nine years.

291
00:15:41,310 --> 00:15:43,641
And the first by a private company.

292
00:15:44,278 --> 00:15:45,032
And Boeing?

293
00:15:45,032 --> 00:15:50,655
Its spacecraft, Starliner, had so many problems on its first crewed flight, in two

294
00:15:50,655 --> 00:15:54,426
thousand twenty-four, that it came back to Earth empty.

295
00:15:54,506 --> 00:16:00,141
Its two astronauts came home in March two thousand twenty-five… aboard a SpaceX

296
00:16:00,141 --> 00:16:03,779
Dragon, after two hundred eighty-six days in space.

297
00:16:04,752 --> 00:16:10,171
In two thousand twenty-one, NASA chose SpaceX's Starship to land astronauts on the

298
00:16:10,171 --> 00:16:14,862
Moon again, with a contract worth two point eight nine billion dollars.

299
00:16:16,231 --> 00:16:21,313
And in April two thousand twenty-six, NASA itself went back to the Moon with the

300
00:16:21,313 --> 00:16:22,584
Artemis two mission:

301
00:16:22,584 --> 00:16:27,476
four astronauts flew around it for the first time since nineteen seventy-two.

302
00:16:27,556 --> 00:16:33,734
And in June two thousand twenty-six, SpaceX, by then merged with Musk's artificial

303
00:16:33,734 --> 00:16:36,296
intelligence company, went public.

304
00:16:36,296 --> 00:16:40,591
It was valued at nearly one point eight trillion dollars.

305
00:16:40,728 --> 00:16:45,699
The company that, in two thousand eight, was one failure away from disappearing had

306
00:16:45,699 --> 00:16:48,395
become one of the most valuable in the world.

307
00:16:48,475 --> 00:16:50,381
So, who owes more to whom?

308
00:16:51,015 --> 00:16:55,744
Without NASA, SpaceX probably wouldn't have survived two thousand eight.

309
00:16:55,744 --> 00:17:00,210
Its money and its contracts gave it a chance when nobody else would.

310
00:17:01,396 --> 00:17:06,578
But without SpaceX, NASA would have spent many more years paying Russia for every

311
00:17:06,578 --> 00:17:06,898
seat.

312
00:17:06,898 --> 00:17:12,144
And its astronauts stranded in two thousand twenty-five would have had to wait for

313
00:17:12,144 --> 00:17:13,360
another spacecraft.

314
00:17:13,440 --> 00:17:15,475
And it all started with a beep.

315
00:17:15,475 --> 00:17:20,859
Fear of a small Soviet metal ball created the agency that took humans to the Moon.

316
00:17:21,400 --> 00:17:26,981
And fifty years later, that same agency used a contract to save the private company

317
00:17:26,981 --> 00:17:29,603
that now takes its astronauts to space.

318
00:17:29,770 --> 00:17:30,891
What do you think?

319
00:17:30,891 --> 00:17:35,873
Was NASA right to bet on private companies, or should space stay in the hands of

320
00:17:35,873 --> 00:17:36,620
governments?

321
00:17:36,620 --> 00:17:38,364
Let me know in the comments.

322
00:17:39,393 --> 00:17:42,060
And if you like these stories, subscribe:

323
00:17:42,060 --> 00:17:45,247
every week, the final balance sheet of a company.

324
00:17:45,247 --> 00:17:47,263
Thanks for watching to the end.

325
00:17:47,263 --> 00:17:48,823
See you in the next one.

